# Project: Cucumber BDD Test Automation Framework

## Framework Structure
- Write feature files in Gherkin (.feature) under a `features/` or `src/test/resources/features/` directory
- Implement step definitions in a dedicated `steps/` or `step_definitions/` package
- Use a support directory (`features/support/` or `src/test/java/.../support/`) for hooks and world objects
- Separate feature files by domain or user story area, not by technical layer

## Coding Standards
- Write Gherkin in plain business language — no technical implementation details in scenarios
- Use consistent verb tenses: "Given" (state), "When" (action), "Then" (outcome)
- Keep step definition methods small and focused; delegate to page objects or service clients
- Name step definition classes by domain (e.g. `CheckoutSteps`, `LoginSteps`)

## Feature File Writing
- Each scenario must be self-contained and executable independently
- Prefer `Scenario Outline` + `Examples` table over duplicated scenarios for data-driven tests
- Use `Background` only for steps shared across every scenario in the file
- Limit scenarios to one behaviour per scenario — avoid long chains of unrelated When/Then steps
- Tag scenarios meaningfully: `@smoke`, `@regression`, `@wip`, `@slow`

```gherkin
# ✅ Good — describes business behaviour, not implementation
Feature: User Authentication
  As a registered user
  I want to log in to my account
  So that I can access my personal dashboard

  Background:
    Given the login page is displayed

  Scenario: Successful login with valid credentials
    When I enter valid credentials
    Then I should be redirected to the dashboard
    And I should see a welcome message

  Scenario Outline: Login fails with invalid credentials
    When I enter "<username>" and "<password>"
    Then I should see the error "<message>"

    Examples:
      | username        | password   | message                    |
      | unknown@test.co | anypass    | User not found             |
      | user@test.co    | wrongpass  | Invalid password           |
      | ""              | ""         | Email and password required|
```

## Step Definitions
- Match steps with unambiguous, reusable patterns — avoid overly specific step text
- Use parameter types (`{string}`, `{int}`, `{word}`) or custom parameter types to capture values
- Never put assertions inside Given steps; reserve assertions for Then steps
- Share state between steps via a World object or dependency injection, never via static fields

```java
// ✅ Java + Cucumber-JVM with PicoContainer injection
@Given("the login page is displayed")
public void theLoginPageIsDisplayed() {
    loginPage.open();
}

@When("I enter valid credentials")
public void iEnterValidCredentials() {
    loginPage.login(testUser.getEmail(), testUser.getPassword());
}

@Then("I should be redirected to the dashboard")
public void iShouldBeRedirectedToTheDashboard() {
    assertThat(dashboardPage.isDisplayed()).isTrue();
}
```

```javascript
// ✅ JavaScript (Cucumber.js) with World context
Given('the login page is displayed', async function () {
  await this.page.goto('/login')
})

When('I enter valid credentials', async function () {
  await this.loginPage.login(this.testUser.email, this.testUser.password)
})

Then('I should be redirected to the dashboard', async function () {
  await expect(this.page).toHaveURL('/dashboard')
})
```

## Hooks
- Use `@Before` and `@After` hooks scoped to tags (`@Before("@browser")`) to avoid unnecessary setup
- Put global teardown (screenshot on failure, session cleanup) in `@After`
- Keep hook logic thin — delegate heavy setup to factory or fixture classes
- Order hooks explicitly when execution sequence matters

## World / Context Objects
- Use a World object (Cucumber.js) or PicoContainer / Spring injection (Cucumber-JVM) for shared state
- Reset all shared state before each scenario — stale state causes false passes
- Never use Cucumber's global state for data that should be scenario-scoped

## Page Objects / Service Clients
- Abstract UI interactions in Page Object classes; step definitions call page objects, not WebDriver directly
- For API testing, use a dedicated API client class accessed from step definitions
- Keep assertions in step definitions, not in page objects

## Test Data Management
- Drive test data through Examples tables or external files (JSON, YAML, CSV)
- Never hardcode environment-specific values (URLs, credentials) in feature files
- Use a data factory or builder pattern for complex domain objects
- Seed and clean up test data in `@Before`/`@After` hooks, not inside steps

## Tags and Filtering
- Run smoke suite: `cucumber --tags "@smoke"`
- Skip work-in-progress: `cucumber --tags "not @wip"`
- Combine tags with `and`/`or`: `cucumber --tags "@regression and not @slow"`
- Define tag conventions in a team-wide `TAGS.md` document

## Reporting
- Generate HTML and JSON reports for CI consumption (Allure, Cucumber HTML Reporter)
- Attach screenshots to failing scenarios in the `@After` hook
- Use `publish: true` (Cucumber.js) or Allure plugin for shareable reports
- Log step context (request/response, page URL) to help diagnose failures

## CI/CD Integration
- Parallelise test execution by feature file or scenario using `--parallel` (Cucumber.js) or Surefire forks (JVM)
- Tag-filter in CI: run `@smoke` on every PR, full `@regression` suite on nightly builds
- Fail the build on undefined or pending steps — never allow them to pass silently
- Store Cucumber JSON reports as CI artifacts for trend analysis

## Performance
- Avoid `Thread.sleep`; use explicit waits or polling for asynchronous UI/API behaviour
- Group slow integration scenarios under `@slow` and exclude them from the fast feedback loop
- Reuse browser sessions across scenarios in the same feature when safe (stateless flows)

## Documentation
- Maintain one English-language feature file per user story or business capability
- Use the Feature header (`As a… / I want… / So that…`) to record the business rationale
- Keep a `README` in the features root explaining the tagging strategy and how to run locally

Remember: Gherkin is a communication tool first and a test script second — every feature file should be readable and meaningful to a non-technical stakeholder.
